  response carries. Smallest possible change, no new files, nothing added to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. The cost lands later: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is
  the login page's script, so a second form on another page either duplicates the store or drags all of [38;5;153mapp.js[39m along.
  You've told me other forms are coming, which is exactly the case this approach is worst at.

  [1mB. A separate [38;5;153msession.js[39m, loaded before [38;5;153mapp.js[0m [3m(recommended)[0m — the store moves to its own file exposing one global
  ([38;5;153mwindow.UserSession[39m), and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains a [38;5;153m<script src="session.js">[39m before the existing tag. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m consumes it and
  otherwise changes the same way as in A. This buys a real module boundary without a bundler or build step: a future
  form includes one script tag and has the ID. The costs are honest and small — one extra file, one extra request, and
  script-tag ordering that must stay correct. Given that a second consumer is a stated requirement rather than a guess,
  I'd pay that now; extracting it later means touching every call site that grew in the meantime.

